A||N||10||bHooper||buying a "seized" vehicle...||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||08-23-2000||02:44 PM||||has anyone here ever bought one of those "seized" vehicles, that you see advertised as:<P>"buy a car for $500... federal and state seized vehicles"<P>the reason i am asking, there are several fiero's listed on Yahoo under this ad.<P>it seems like they want you to sign up for a service... is this legit/legal/real?<P>can you really get a deal like this?  are they just making it easier than actually going to the local police auctions?<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||08-23-2000||05:53 PM||||Sure you can buy some cars for $500.  Great things like Yugos, Honda Civics, etc.  Most of the nicer cars are much more expensive.  Hey.  It's an auction.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||08-23-2000||06:32 PM||||I might be a little suspicious about them.  A couple of months ago, I was thinking of getting a 1995 Chevrolet Caprice w/ a 9C1 police package.  So, I e-mailed them about ten times, didn't get a reply once.  I went on the website they had; it says to buy a book full of all the auctioned vehicles in the area.  I don't know.  Remember:  If it's too good to be true, it probably is.||||reg||4
Z||000003||mrfiero||08-23-2000||06:40 PM||||Those ads you see in the papers and magazines are not advertising a particular auction at all.  They are advertising their service to tell you where the auctions are......they charge you for something you can do for yourself!<P>These places are not affiliated with the auction houses or anything else for that matter.....they just research the auctions and sell the info to unsuspecting people who don't know any better.  <P>If you want to go to an auction you need to familiarize yourself with the auction houses in your area.  There are several here in Denver (I've been to a few)....but believe me, you won't find any "steals".||||reg||4
Z||000004||overlord||08-23-2000||06:51 PM||||my uncle bought a car from one of those auctions......he got like a 30,000 car for 8000.......||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||08-23-2000||07:50 PM||||When I was looking for a house, I called about the seized homes for "pennies on the dollar." The guy wanted to sell a subscription to a service that publishes the monthly auction listings. Being the smarta$$ that I am, I said to send it to me and if I used it to buy a house I would gladly pay for the service. He declined, so I just hung up. It is a scam, plain and simple.||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||08-23-2000||08:22 PM||||thats just what i thought... a scam, selling you what you could find if yoiu looked hard enough for yourself!<P>but they did have a '86 GT they were advertising ofr around $600!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||2
Z||000007||mrfiero||08-23-2000||08:45 PM||||Many times they list cars with great prices as a "hook" to get you to buy their service.  Odds are good that the '86 GT either doesn't exist or will go for a much higher price.<P>I don't dispute that good deals can be had at auctions, just that it is a rare occurance.  I went to an auction a few years ago because it had an '88 coupe.  It was in fair shape, nothing special....I had planned to go as high as $500.  I was a few minutes late and by the time I got there it was well over $1000.....definitely more than it was worth.  I believe it finally went for $1200.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Oreif||08-24-2000||02:15 AM||||When I was in Florida, A friend went to a police auction and bought a 1 year old Porsche 911. He paid $2500.00 (yes the decimal is in the right place) But the catch is the vehicle was seized in a drug raid and the police dis-assembled the vehicle looking for drugs and contraband. He bought the car totally in peices. After he got it to his garage he found out that they were not very careful in the dis-assembly. 2 years and $30,000.00 later it was back together. Not bad if you want to spend the extra money and time. I have heard that most vehicles are sold for close to the market value. $500 would probably get you an escort that some punk thought he could out run a police car with, until that tree jumped out in front of him.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||whamel||08-24-2000||06:40 AM||||Last time I looked at one of these where they listed specific vehicles, the guy told me that those are 'typical example cars and prices that they have gone for.'  I actually sent for the list, but unfortunately it didn't do me a lot of good since there wasn't a single listed auction site within 250 miles of where I lived.  Oh well, live and learn.<BR>Now I just prefer to go to the local police and dealer auctions.  I get better deals there anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Xtreme Fiero's||08-24-2000||06:14 PM||||My old boss bought a 97 Jeep Grand Cherokee for $1,500 at onw of those, but go to one in person so you can see the car so you don't get riped off/||||reg||1
